t on  conditions and organize a prisoner  movement to fight back against the nazis  of course he would also be trapped in a  place dedicated to brutality and death  but that clearly wasn't a significant  enough deterrent to make him change his  mind  world war ii kicked off with germany's  invasion of poland on the 1st of  september 1939  and poland's defense was spirited but  short-lived in fact the country never  officially surrendered but after soviet  forces invaded from the east on the 17th  of september the battle was clearly lost  pilecki was ordered to flee his native  country but instead he remains there to  continue the underground fight against  the invaders  he was responsible for jointly founding  one of the first resistance movements  the secret polish army which was well  polish  and a secret  at its height the organization had close  to 20 000 members and it was eventually  absorbed into the polish home army  a colossal underground network of  freedom fighters who had no intention of  accepting the rule of their new  occupiers on the 19th of september 1940  using fake identity documents polesky  allowed himself to be rounded up and  arrested by the nazis  that was the easy part in those days all  you needed to get yourself arrested was  a face  the hard part was still to come and soon  enough polesky was sent to auschwitz  where he became prisoner 4859  it's hard to imagine what was going  through the man's mind when the train  first arrived at the camp no doubt he'd  been expecting conditions to be bad but  auschwitz was still relatively new at  the time and details on what really went  on in the camp were scanned  deliberately getting himself arrested  was undeniably an incredibly brave thing  to do  but in truth polesky would have no idea  of the sheer horrors that awaited him in  his new life  not that it took long for him to get a  taste he and his fellow prisoners were  beaten with clubs the moment they  stepped down from the train  then their 