on if only he could speak to his  superiors in person  of course there was only one way to do  that  he was going to have to escape from  auschwitz  prisoners at the camp were beaten  humiliated and very often executed even  for minor infractions and often for no  reason at all being caught attempting to  escape was quite simply a death sentence  but if he wanted to help his fellow  prisoners pileski had no choice but to  try  so  one april night in 1943 he and two of  his fellow prisoners pried open a metal  door at the bakery where they were  working the night shift  despite their weakened states they were  able to subdue a guard before locking  him in a woodshed  they also had the presence of mind to  sever a phone line and disable an alarm  before escaping on foot taking stolen  german identity documents with them  it would be several days almost 100  kilometers and at least one close call  with german soldiers during which  poleski was shot and wounded before the  men reached the security of a home army  safe house  outside occupied warsaw  there pileski started work on what's now  known as withhold report which focused  on the zow and his experiences in  auschwitz the purpose of the report was  to convince the home army to attempt to  liberate the camp  but  the organization concluded that even if  they were to attack auschwitz they  didn't have the resources to transport  the prisoners to safety despite the  setback pileski continued to fight for  both the resistance and for his former  inmates in auschwitz coordinating  activities between the zow and the home  army as best as he could  by february 1944 he was promoted to the  rank of captain and in the summer of the  following year he took part in the  warsaw uprising during which the home  army attempted to oust the occupying  germans  pileski was actually supposed to remain  out of harm's way during the uprising  but true to form he entered the conflict  anonymously and fought as a regular  soldier unsurprisingly he excelled and  as polish casualties began to mount he  revealed his true identity and rank to  his superior officers who immediately  put him in command of his own unit  after 63 days of brutal fighting the  warsaw uprising ended in failure and  polesky was one of the many poles forced  to surrender to the nazis  he was taken to a pow camp in bavaria  where he would spend the rest of the war  until the camp's liberation in april  1945  but whilst the second world war came to  an end for most people in 1945  things were a little different in poland  red army soldiers still occupied the  country and stalin orchestrated the  installation of a soviet-backed puppet  government that transformed poland into  little more than a vassal state of the  soviet union  after a brief stint in italy poleski was  ordered to go undercover in his native  country to gather intelligence on the  activities of the occupying soviets  arriving in warsaw in december 1945 he  recruited members from the zow and the  home army to form a new intelligence  network though it was probably a walk in  the park compared to auschwitz pileski's  time undercover was still incredibly  dangerous and he was forced to change  identities and jobs on a regular basis  in order to avoid discovery  those precautions worked for a while but  in 1946 he received a tip-off that his  cover had been compromised he was  ordered to pull out of poland  immediately  but he refused to abandon his homeland  polesky was arrested by the soviets on  the 8th of may 1947.  he was interrogated and tortured for  upwards of a year by men who were well  known for their cruelty  but  he never talked  [Music]  when it became clear he wasn't going to  give up any valuable information on the  polish resistance poleski was put on  trial where he was accused of a battery  of charges against a puppet state  including illegal border crossing the  use of forged documents espionage and  planning to assassinate several senior  politicians  he pleaded guilty to all but two of the  charges he denied espionage on the  grounds that he was an officer of the  second polish corps and therefore wasn't  breaking any laws and he denied the  charge of planning assassinations for  the simple reason that they weren't true  not that any of it mattered the trial  was a sham and there is only going to  ever be one outcome  polesky was found guilty on all counts  he was executed on the 25th of may 1948.  to this day nobody knows where his body  is buried  [Music]  until 1989 captain pileski's remarkable  story was censored in poland  but after the collapse of communism his  trial was officially reviewed and the  sentence quashed  in 1995 he was posthumously awarded the  order of polonia restitutor and in 2006  the order of the white eagle the highest  decoration in all of poland  these medals probably wouldn't have  meant much to a man who had fought so  bravely for his country's liberty  but still  it does feel like justice was eventually  served  [Music]  thanks for watching
 

